<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry III, Holy Roman Emperor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1016">1016</a>–<a href="/years/1056">1056</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Henry the Black · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salian-dynasty">Salian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry III, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salian-dynasty">Salian dynasty</a>, was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> from 1039 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> from 1046. At the Synod of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutri">Sutri</a> in 1046 he deposed rival popes and went on to install reforming German popes. He favoured the palace at Goslar, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rammelsberg">Rammelsberg</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines, where he founded the collegiate church of Saints Simon and Jude; his heart was buried there after his death in 1056.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-iii-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="Henry III, Holy Roman Emperor" width="320" height="700" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heinrich_III._(HRR)_Miniatur.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Henry was the eldest son of Conrad II and Gisela of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a>. Conrad, a Franconian aristocrat with lands along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, was illiterate, but Gisela, who was descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, saw to her son's education and Henry learnt to read. His father made him Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in 1026 and had him elected co-ruler in 1028, when Pilgrim, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, crowned him king at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a>. In 1038 he received the duchy of Swabia and the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. His succession on his father's death was unusually smooth, and he also took the duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Henry began with a tour of his domains and made Goslar a future imperial residence. His first campaign, into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> in 1040, ended in an ambush at the Battle of Brůdek and a heavy retreat, but a second campaign a year later brought a peace with Duke Bretislav I of Bohemia at Regensburg. In 1044 he entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-orseolo">Peter Orseolo</a> to the throne after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-aba">Samuel Aba</a> had overthrown him. In 1043 he married Agnes of Poitou, despite clerical objections to their kinship. He treated the duchies as royal offices to be filled at his discretion, which led to long conflict in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> with Godfrey, the son of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gothelo-i-duke-of-lorraine">Gothelo I</a>.</p><p>In 1046 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to deal with three men who claimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-benedict-ix">Benedict IX</a>, Sylvester III and Gregory VI. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutri">Sutri</a>, on 20 December 1046, he deposed all three. He had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-ii">Suidger</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a> acclaimed as Pope Clement II, and on Christmas Day 1046 Clement crowned Henry and Agnes emperor and empress. The Roman citizenry gave Henry the Golden Chain of the Patriciate and the rank of patricius.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Henry's last years brought setbacks. In 1051 he suffered a major defeat at the Battle of Vértes in a third Hungarian campaign, and in 1052 he failed to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Pressburg</a>, after which he never tried again. A Swabian army sent to help Pope Leo in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> was cut to pieces by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> at the Battle of Civitate on 18 June 1053. Revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> followed the deposition of Duke Cuno.</p><p>His son Henry, born in 1050, was elected king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> at Tribur on 26 June 1053 and crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> on 17 July 1054. In 1055 the heir was engaged to Bertha of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>. Henry III died on 5 October 1056, aged only 39. Modern historians see the last years of his reign as the beginning of a crisis in the Salian monarchy, and the dispute over investiture overshadowed the reigns of his son and grandson.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henry III, Holy Roman Emperor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After his son Godfrey the Bearded rebelled against Emperor <b>Henry III</b>, the emperor gave Upper Lorraine in 1047 to Adalbert and, after Adalbert’s death in 1048, to his kinsman Gerard of Alsace.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Lorraine · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom defended itself successfully against the Holy Roman Empire: Conrad II's invasion of 1030 failed, and although <b>Henry III</b> restored King Peter Orseolo after the Battle of Ménfő in 1044, his campaigns of 1051 and 1052, including a siege of Pressburg, ended in failure.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1000-1301">Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Henry III</b> had been king of Germany since 1039.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>Silver was found in the Rammelsberg near Goslar in the 10th century, and the Salian emperors built an imperial palace there, where <b>Henry III</b> often resided.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-goslar">Free Imperial City of Goslar</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Bodfeld, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.08,11.39&amp;year=1042" aria-label="Bavaria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4813 451 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Henry III, Holy Roman Emperor"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5263 721h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5263 721h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.08,11.39&amp;year=1042">What was here in 1042? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
